From 2ca3ff0d83692e50cf4574ba4cb864c43ed226e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:40:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVLayerMerge: pair two tracks whose decoding times are a
 constant apart

The join of a Dolby Vision stream in two tracks took the access unit of
a base packet to be the enhancement packet with the same decoding time.
Two tracks can have the same pictures in the same order and a decoding
delay of their own: MP4 gives each track its own decoding times, and a
disc may be so (CroqueMr found such a case after a seek in his build).
Paired by equal decoding times, every base packet then carried the
enhancement access unit of another picture, and where the enhancement
layer was ahead its first access units after the start or a seek were
dropped, its random-access picture with them: no full enhancement layer
until its next random-access picture. Measured with made-up packets
against the join as it was: 0 of 48 base units with their own
enhancement unit at one or two pictures of offset, either way.

The offset is now learned at a base packet of a random-access picture
when the enhancement track has a packet of a random-access picture
presented at the same time, and only when the CONFIRM (4) access units
after each are pairwise presented at the same times and of the same
unit by that offset; the base packet waits for those packets where it
is not the offset in use. Where the enhancement packet it pairs with
now is no random-access one (an enhancement layer behind in decoding
time brings the packet of the same picture later), the base packet
waits for the enhancement packets of up to that second after its
decoding time; where both are random-access ones, nothing waits. The
enhancement packets then pair by their decoding time less the offset. Two layers coded in different orders (the
two-track test stream: the same decoding times, and random-access
pictures of the two layers in other access units, one of them presented
at the time of one of the other layer) give no such run: nothing is
learned, and they pair as before. Without decoding times (Matroska)
nothing changes. The offset stays across a seek, is checked again at
the next such picture, and goes with Restart(); at most one second.

kodi.log says it once it is not 0 (and when it changes, four times a
pair at most): "Dolby Vision in two tracks: the decoding times of the
enhancement track (stream N) are X ms before/after those of the base
track ...", and the line of the counts gives the offset in use, how
many were learned and how many pairs of random-access pictures taught
nothing.
